CREATED:20260504T223130Z
DESCRIPTION:The aim of this Talk is to report some predictions in a variet
 y of materials and arising from the use of first-principle and first\npri
 nciple-based numerical approaches.\nMore precisely\, four different topic
 s will be discussed in detail\, if time allows:\n- 1) a design of multifu
 nctional materials having several optimized physical properties\, includi
 ng strong piezoelectricity\, large\nelectro-optic conversion\, as well as
  ultrahigh energy density and efficiency [1\,2].\n- 2) a proposed way to 
 induce the elusive quantum spin liquid state in 2D magnets [3\,4].\n- 3) 
 the predictions that complex ferroelectric bulks and nanostructures can e
 xhibit a variety of so-called hidden states (i.e.\, states\nthat are not 
 accessible via the tuning of thermodynamic variables\, such as temperatur
 e or pressure) with enhanced properties\, and\nare promising for ultrafas
 t neuromorphic computing\, when applying THz pulses of electric fields [5
 \,6].\n- 4) the occurrence of unusual phenomena when twisted light is app
 lied to ferroelectrics [7\,8].\nReferences:\n[1] Z. Jiang et al\, Phys. R
 ev. Lett. 123\, 096801 (2019).\n[2] Z. Jiang et al\, Phys. Rev. Mater. 5\
 , L072401 (2021).\n[3] C. Xu et al\, npj Comp. Mat. 4\, 57 (2018).\n[4] C
 . Xu et al\, Phys. Rev. Lett. 124\, 087205 (2020).\n[5] S. Prosandeev et 
 al.\, Phys. Rev. Lett. 126\, 027602 (2021).
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260504T223130Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260507T113000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260504T223130Z
